2</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.287</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.332</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.361</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.586</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.433</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.692</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.766</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="nt101"><p><italic>NWD</italic> loci were sliced as the three functional domain encoding sequences, and successive 750bp regions upstream and downstream of the genes were analysed. Significantly lower values at a 99&#x00025; confidence interval are bold. As a control, CA/TA and TG/TA ratios for 20 different randomly selected 750bp regions of <italic>P. anserina</italic> genome were 2.244&#x0002b;/&#x02212; 0.854 and 2.225&#x0002b;/&#x02212; 0.992 respectively. Low ratios downstream of <italic>HNWD1</italic> appear to result from RIP of a repeated sequence related to a <italic>Tad3-2</italic> LINE element and sequences upstream of HNWD3 match <italic>P. anserina</italic> centromeric DNA.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="pone-0000283-t002" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000283.t002</object-id><label>Table 2</label><caption><title>WD-repeat number polymorphism.</title></caption><graphic id="pone-0000283-t002-2" xlink:href="pone.0000283.t002"/><table frame="hsides" rules="groups" alternate-form-of="pone-0000283-t002-2"><colgroup span="1"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/></colgroup><thead><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td colspan="7" align="left" rowspan="1">Wild P. anserina isolates</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Loci</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">D</td><td 